  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDewpoints will surge into the lower 70s today adding to the surface \nbased CAPE for the region with a prefrontal trough that will also be \nworking its way into and through the CWA. Meanwhile, a weak upper \nlevel trough axis will also track into the area from the west, \naiding in the formation of a subtle surface low moving through \nnorthern Ohio towards western Pennsylvania later this afternoon. As \nconvection fires, development of this surface low could aid in \nenhancement of it and/or add to additional areal coverage heading \ninto the evening hours for the eastern CWA. Off the surface, low \nlevel jet enhancement also occurs after 21Z today while 0-6km shear \nincreases to 45-55kts in this setup, supporting line segment/QLCS \nformation. Models also continue to show a dry layer of air between \naround 600-650mb with the CAPE stretching into and above this layer, \nadding a mechanism to enhance downdrafts and a damaging wind threat \ncoupled with the low mid level flow in place. Going back to the \naforementioned surface low development, this adds a backing element \nto the surface winds and adds to the low level helicity variable, so \nisolated tornadoes cannot be ruled out. As the convection exits \novernight, another upper level trough axis digs into the southern \nGreat Lakes from the west keeping shower and storm chances going in \nthe southern zones Thursday as the frontal boundary lingers over \ncentral/southern Ohio.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure moves in for Friday into the weekend, but weakness in \nthe upper level mass fields brings back chances for showers/storms \nlate Saturday and through early next week as the pattern becomes \nmore unsettled once again. Low end POPs Sunday through Monday become \nmore numerous and greater in coverage ahead of the next cold front \naround the Tuesday time frame. After cooler end of the week \nconditions, temperatures will push back into the mid 80s by Tuesday \nwhile dewpoint Sunday through Tuesday will be back into the upper \n60s to lower 70s.",
